In speech synthesis, researchers commonly aim to create generated speech that appears more natural and appealing to listeners. Despite the existing research, there is still limited understanding of how people evaluate synthesized speech, particularly when it comes to assessing its emotional authenticity. This study examines the emotional attributes of female speech and develops a technique to measure how much a synthesized voice possesses a specific emotional characteristic.
The researchers discovered that certain emotional attributes were linked to specific acoustic features of speech, and they developed a scoring system for recognizing emotional attributes in speech. They also found that combining emotional characteristics in specific proportions (convex combinations) significantly influenced how competent female speakers were perceived, with optimal levels of competence situated around mid to upper ranges of the possible scores (around the 50th to the 75th percentiles).
Building on these findings, the researchers created "affect-adjusted" versions of existing female voices that were then combined with human speech to create new voices. These modifications enhanced the ability of female speakers to effectively communicate emotional authenticity and increased the listeners' sense of the speaker's competence, as assessed above the average of the possible scores (above the 50th percentile). The adjustments also benefited the listeners' understanding of the speaker's emotional state and reduced the listeners' sense of the speech being overly artificial, while maximizing the listeners' sense of the speech being coherent and natural in tone. The most recent version of the HyperVoice Josie voice, which underwent the affect-adjusting process, can be accessed at www.hypervoice.com. The researchers' scoring system, data on the relationship between emotional characteristics and acoustic features, and the code for their synthesis evaluation algorithm have been made publicly available on the GitHub repository at https://github.com/m/emi-hys. This repository also contains the code for the affect-adjusting technique used in this investigation.}
